---
import LongTailCalculator from '../components/LongTailCalculator.astro';

const fields = [
  { id: 'length', label: 'Length (ft)', type: 'number', min: 0.5, step: 0.1, value: '10' },
  { id: 'width', label: 'Width (ft)', type: 'number', min: 0.5, step: 0.1, value: '10' },
  { id: 'thickness', label: 'Thickness (in)', type: 'number', min: 2, step: 0.5, value: '4' },
  { id: 'price', label: 'Ready-mix price ($/cu yd)', type: 'number', min: 50, step: 5, value: '150' },
  { id: 'waste', label: 'Waste (%)', type: 'number', min: 0, max: 30, step: 1, value: '10' },
];

const results = [
  { key: 'cubicYards', label: 'Concrete needed', unit: 'cu yd', format: 'fixed2' },
  { key: 'eightyPoundBags', label: '80 lb bag equivalent' },
  { key: 'materialCost', label: 'Material cost', format: 'money' },
];
---

<LongTailCalculator
  title="Concrete Slab Cost Calculator"
  description="Enter slab size, thickness and your local ready-mix price to get cubic yards, bag equivalent and total material cost, with a per-square-foot cost table."
  slug="concrete-slab-cost-calculator"
  calcType="concrete-slab-cost"
  quickAnswer="A 10x10 slab at 4 inches thick needs about 1.36 cubic yards with 10% waste. At $150 per cubic yard, ready-mix material costs about $204 — or $3.89–$5.00 per square foot if you mix bags instead."
  formula="Volume = length × width × (thickness ÷ 12) ÷ 27. Cost = cubic yards × price per cubic yard."
  fields={fields}
  results={results}
  table={{
    caption: 'Concrete slab material cost at $150 per cubic yard, including 10% waste.',
    headers: ['Slab size', 'Thickness', 'Cu yd', '80 lb bags', 'Material cost'],
    rows: [
      ['4 x 4', '4"', '0.22', '10', '$33'],
      ['8 x 8', '4"', '0.87', '40', '$130'],
      ['10 x 10', '4"', '1.36', '62', '$204'],
      ['10 x 10', '6"', '2.03', '92', '$305'],
      ['12 x 12', '4"', '1.96', '89', '$294'],
      ['20 x 20', '4"', '5.43', '245', '$815'],
      ['20 x 20', '6"', '8.15', '367', '$1,222'],
      ['24 x 24', '6"', '11.73', '528', '$1,760'],
    ],
  }}
  note="This is material cost only. Add grading, forms, wire mesh, delivery, and finishing labor separately."
  guide={[
    {
      heading: 'Cost per square foot, by thickness',
      paragraphs: [
        'Per-square-foot pricing is how contractors quote, so it is worth knowing the conversion. One cubic yard covers <b>81 square feet at 4 inches</b>, 64.8 square feet at 5 inches, and 54 square feet at 6 inches. At 2026 ready-mix prices of $150–$200 per yard delivered, the material alone works out to:',
        '<b>4 inches:</b> $1.85–$2.47 per square foot &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>5 inches:</b> $2.31–$3.09 &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>6 inches:</b> $2.78–$3.70.',
        'Mixing the same yard from bags at $7–$9 per 80 lb bag (45 bags per yard) lands at $3.89–$5.00 per square foot at 4 inches — roughly double the ready-mix price, before you spend the five hours it takes to mix and place it. That gap is the whole reason slabs over about 1.5 cubic yards go by truck.',
        'Read those numbers as a decision, not a quote: at 4 inches a 200 square foot patio is $370–$495 in ready-mix material against $780–$1,000 in bags. If your plant quotes $200 a yard and the bags quote comes in closer, someone is comparing a 5-inch pour against a 4-inch one — normalize thickness first, then price.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Buying bags vs ordering ready-mix',
      paragraphs: [
        'The crossover sits near 1.5 cubic yards. Below it, bags win because the truck charges a short-load fee of $100–$250 under a full load; above it, delivered concrete wins on price, labor and set time. For the bag-side arithmetic — <a href="/how-many-bags-of-concrete-in-a-yard/">how many bags of concrete in a yard</a> (45 of 80 lb, 60 of 60 lb, 72 of 50 lb, 90 of 40 lb) — the full table is on its own page.',
        'To price your exact pour bag-by-bag, the <a href="/concrete-bags-calculator/">how many bags of concrete do I need calculator</a> returns the count for any slab size with waste included, and the <a href="/concrete-calculator/">concrete calculator</a> converts any footprint to cubic yards first if you want the volume before the money.',
        'One more line item bags hide: time. Sixty-two bags for a 10x10 slab is a solid half-day of mixing with a rented mixer, and the first batch is setting while the last one mixes. A truck pours the same slab in twenty minutes.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Labor cost: what finishing actually runs',
      paragraphs: [
        'Material is roughly half the bill on a hired-out slab. Finished flatwork labor in 2026 runs <b>$6–$12 per square foot</b> nationally, and it swings hard by region: $5–$8 is common across the South and Midwest, while coastal and metro markets run $9–$15. A 400 square foot driveway slab at $8 per square foot is about $3,200 in labor against $600–$800 in material.',
        'DIY drops that line to zero — but forms, screeding and finishing are skills, and a badly finished 20x20 slab cannot be undone. The honest middle path many homeowners take: pour small slabs yourself, hire the crew for anything over about 200 square feet or any slab that carries a car.',
        'Add-on fees to ask about before you order: short-load charges under a full truck ($100–$250), a pump truck when the truck cannot reach the forms ($30–$60 per yard), and weekend or winter pour premiums where they apply.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What a full quote actually itemizes',
      paragraphs: [
        'When you compare contractor bids on the same slab, line them up line-by-line: concrete (yards × price), delivery and short-load fees, forms and stakes, wire mesh or rebar ($0.10–$0.30 per square foot for mesh, more for rebar), finish type (broom, hard-trowel or stamped — the last one triples the price), sealers, and cleanup or backfill. Two bids that look $1,000 apart are often quoting different finish grades, not different prices for the same work.',
        'A bid that quotes only per-square-foot all-in is still fine — just confirm which thickness, which finish and whose subgrade prep it assumes before you sign. The cheapest bid on paper is usually the one that assumed someone else prepares the base.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'How to reduce slab cost without weakening it',
      paragraphs: [
        'Keep the subgrade flat and compacted. Concrete does not compensate for bad base preparation; it simply cracks over soft spots.',
        'Use the correct thickness for the load. A 4-inch slab is standard for patios and walkways, while driveways often need 5 to 6 inches and reinforcement. Ordering thinner than the load needs is the most expensive saving there is.',
        'Get three quotes. Ready-mix plants vary more on price than on quality, and the spread between the highest and lowest quote on the same pour is routinely 20%.',
      ],
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      question: 'How much does a 10x10 concrete slab cost in materials?',
      answer: 'About $204 in ready-mix at $150 per cubic yard, or about $434 if you use 62 eighty-pound bags at $7 each.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How much does a 20x20 slab cost to pour?',
      answer: 'About $815 in material at 4 inches (5.43 cubic yards at $150) — but with a crew at $6–$12 per square foot labor, a hired-out 20x20 runs roughly $2,400–$4,800 all-in. DIY drops it to the material line plus forms and mesh.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Is bagged concrete cheaper than ready-mix?',
      answer: 'Not for large slabs. Bagged concrete usually costs $11 to $15 per cubic foot, while ready-mix is $5 to $7 per cubic foot before delivery.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How much does thickness affect cost?',
      answer: 'Cost rises almost linearly with thickness. A 6-inch slab uses 50% more concrete than a 4-inch slab of the same size — the table above shows the jump on identical footprints.',
    },
    {
      question: 'What price should I use for ready-mix?',
      answer: 'Use your local supplier’s quoted price. In 2026, typical ready-mix ranges from $125 to $200 per cubic yard before delivery, and most plants add a $100–$250 short-load fee under a full truck.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Does the slab cost include finishing and sealing?',
      answer: 'No — this calculator is material only. Finishing labor runs $6–$12 per square foot hired out, and a curing-and-seal product adds about $0.15–$0.30 per square foot if you apply it yourself.',
    },
  ]}
  related={[
    { label: 'How many bags of concrete do I need', href: '/concrete-bags-calculator/' },
    { label: 'Concrete bag coverage', href: '/concrete-bag-coverage-calculator/' },
    { label: 'Concrete calculator', href: '/concrete-calculator/' },
    { label: 'Concrete for fence posts', href: '/concrete-fence-post-calculator/' },
  ]}
/>
